# Code Comments And Documentation

## Agent obligation (MANDATORY)

**Cursor agents and automated assistants MUST follow this rule** for every new or changed function, class, module, API surface, and UI component. **MUST NOT** omit **Inputs and outputs**, **AI-friendly patterns**, or **Principles** requirements below unless the project overlay explicitly allows a shorter template for a trivial case.

**Repository specifics:** This rule defines company-wide policy. For this project's languages, commands, test/coverage invocation, logging entry points, and default task ID prefix, see [`project-runtime-conventions.mdc`](project-runtime-conventions.mdc) (create or update that file when bootstrapping the repo).

Agents MUST apply this rule to every new or changed function, class, module, API surface, and UI component.

## Audience: humans and AI agents

Agents MUST ensure documentation works for **code review and maintenance** and for **retrieval-augmented editing**: agents often see **partial files**, **search hits**, or **symbol-only** context. Agents MUST put **durable, specific facts** in docstrings and module headers—not only narrative prose.

**Agents rely especially on:**

- The **first paragraph** of a module or public type (often the only chunk retrieved).
- **Explicit nouns**: integration names, env vars, table names, route paths, error codes—spelled in full once where they matter.
- **Boundaries**: what this unit **does not** do, what **calls** it, and what it **must not** import or assume.
- **Stable section labels** inside longer docstrings (see below) so searches like `Raises:` or `Side effects:` land on the right line.

## AI-friendly patterns (REQUIRED)

These rules are **mandatory** for new or changed code. They apply together with **Principles** and **Inputs and outputs** below.

**Trivial files:** For tiny modules (for example pure re-export barrels under ~15 lines), you MAY satisfy this section with **one** compact paragraph that states **Role** and explicitly names **Not in this file**, **Key dependencies**, and **See also** as `None` or “not applicable” when that is true. Anything larger MUST use the structured bullets.

### Module / file header (top of file)

Every **module file** MUST include a module-level docstring (or language-appropriate equivalent) whose **first paragraph** is the summary of responsibility. Immediately after that summary (same docstring), you MUST cover all of the following—use the exact labels **`Role:`**, **`Not in this file:`**, **`Key dependencies:`**, **`See also:`** so tools and agents can grep them:

- **`Role:`** — MUST: one sentence describing what this file provides compared to sibling or related modules.
- **`Not in this file:`** — MUST: what is intentionally out of scope or owned elsewhere; if there is no ambiguity, state **`None`** (or one line: “No boundary confusion with sibling modules.”).
- **`Key dependencies:`** — MUST: named external systems (queues, buckets, APIs) and in-repo modules that are **non-obvious** from imports alone; if none beyond ordinary imports, state **`None`**.
- **`See also:`** — MUST: repo-relative paths to paired tests, OpenAPI or schema files, or the feature **`README.md`** when they exist; if nothing to cite, state **`None`**.

### Docstring sections agents search for

Use the project’s standard format (Google/NumPy/Javadoc—see overlay). You MUST use these **exact section labels** when the condition holds (so searches for `Raises:` and `Side effects:` match):

| Section | MUST include when |
|--------|-------------------|
| **Raises** / **throws** | Callers must catch, translate, or otherwise handle **specific** documented failures (not every possible runtime error). |
| **Side effects** | The code mutates DB, cache, files, global config, emits events, or has other observable external effects. |
| **Yields** | The callable is a generator or async iterator. |
| **Note** or **Warning** | Security, idempotency, threading, or ordering constraints apply. |

For **non-obvious algorithms**, you MUST include at least one line on **invariant** or **complexity** (not only control-flow narration).

### Cross-references

- You MUST include **`See also:`** with **`path/to/module.py`** or repo-relative paths when an architectural relationship matters and is not obvious from imports alone.
- HTTP handlers MUST document **`operationId`** and/or the **route path** in the docstring when the handler name does not mirror the URL.

### What not to do (MUST NOT)

- MUST NOT use **tickets only** in place of intent (`TODO JIRA-1` without a one-line description of what is missing).
- MUST NOT paste **large** OpenAPI/JSON schema bodies into comments—link or point to the schema file path in **`See also:`**.

## Principles (REQUIRED)

1. Agents MUST prefer **clear names and structure** over comments; agents MUST add comments where intent, constraints, or non-obvious behavior would otherwise be lost.
2. Comments MUST stay **accurate**: agents MUST update or remove them when code changes. Stale comments are worse than none.
3. Agents MUST NOT use comments to hide **secrets**, tokens, or real PII (same bar as logging—see [`logging-and-data-safety.mdc`](logging-and-data-safety.mdc)).
4. Agents MUST avoid **noise**: MUST NOT restate what the code literally does line by line.
5. Agents MUST use **English** for all public-facing documentation unless the project explicitly standardizes on another language.

### Inputs and outputs (REQUIRED)

Documentation for **public** surfaces MUST include a **clear description of inputs and outputs**, not only purpose. Agents MUST use the repository’s standard doc format (for example Google-style **Args** / **Returns**, NumPy-style **Parameters** / **Returns**, Javadoc `@param` / `@return`, etc.—see overlay in [`project-runtime-conventions.mdc`](project-runtime-conventions.mdc) when set).

- **Functions and methods (public):** Agents MUST document parameters (or “none”) and return value or **Yields** (or “none” / `None`). Callers MUST NOT be forced to infer contracts from types alone when names or generics are ambiguous.
- **HTTP/RPC handlers:** Agents MUST document inputs (route/query/body/auth context) and outputs (response shape and status semantics) when not fully defined by OpenAPI/IDL alone.
- **UI components:** Agents MUST document **props (inputs)** and **what the component renders or exposes** (DOM structure at a high level, emitted events, or slot content)—not only “what it is for.”
- **Custom hooks:** Agents MUST document parameters, return value (tuple or object shape), and side effects.
- **Private** helpers: a one-line summary is enough; agents MUST add explicit inputs/outputs when the signature is non-obvious or overloaded.

## Backend (services, APIs, jobs, libraries)

Applies to Python, Go, Java, C#, Kotlin, Ruby, etc., in whatever form the stack uses (docstrings, Javadoc, XML docs, etc.).

### Modules and packages

- Agents MUST start each **module file** with a short description of its responsibility (one to three sentences or a tight paragraph), and satisfy **`Role:`** / **`Not in this file:`** / **`Key dependencies:`** / **`See also:`** per **AI-friendly patterns (REQUIRED)** above.
- Agents MUST call out **non-obvious** side effects (network, disk, environment, global state) at module or entry-point level when relevant.

### Functions and methods

- **Public** functions and methods (exported API, HTTP handlers, RPC, jobs): agents MUST document **purpose**, **inputs** (parameters, context, environment), **outputs** (return value, side effects on returned data), and **errors** / **Raises** where callers must handle failures. Types complement but MUST NOT replace these descriptions when behavior or semantics matter.
- Agents MUST use the project’s standard doc format consistently (for example **Google-style** or **NumPy-style** docstrings in Python; **Javadoc**/**KDoc** in JVM; **Go doc** comments above the declaration), including explicit **Args**/**Parameters** and **Returns** sections (or equivalent) unless the project overlay specifies a shorter template for trivial cases.
- Agents MUST include **Raises** / **throws** / **errors** for failure modes that callers must handle.
- **Private** helpers: a one-line summary is enough unless the algorithm, invariant, or performance tradeoff needs explanation; agents MUST add inputs/outputs when the contract is not obvious from the signature.
- **Complex** logic: agents MUST prefer a short block comment above the block explaining **why** the approach or **what invariant** is maintained—not a transcript of the control flow.

### Types and data shapes

- Agents MUST prefer **named types** and **schema definitions** (OpenAPI, Pydantic, TypeScript types) over duplicating the same shape in prose.
- When types are insufficient, agents MUST document **semantic** constraints (for example value ranges, ordering, idempotency).

### Endpoints and integrations

- Route or controller handlers: agents MUST state **auth requirements**, **idempotency**, and **important side effects** if not obvious from middleware or OpenAPI.
- External integrations: agents MUST document **timeouts**, **retries**, and **assumptions** about the remote system in one place (module or small doc), not scattered one-liners.

## Frontend (components, views, hooks, styles)

Applies to React, Vue, Svelte, Angular, and similar UI stacks.

### Components (functions, classes, or framework-specific units)

- Each **component** MUST have a brief description of **what it renders or owns**, **when** to use it versus alternatives, and—explicitly—**inputs (props)** and **outputs** (rendered UI, forwarded refs, or events/callbacks invoked).
- Agents MUST document **props** with types and short descriptions for each meaningful prop (required vs optional, defaults, controlled vs uncontrolled). Agents MUST treat **outputs** as what the user or parent receives: visible UI role, emitted events, or exposed imperative handle—whichever applies.
- For **shared** or **library** components, agents MUST document **accessibility** expectations (labels, keyboard, focus) when non-default.
- Agents MUST NOT comment every JSX line; agents MUST comment **structure** (sections), **conditional UI** that encodes product rules, and **hacks** or **browser** workarounds.

### Hooks and state

- Custom **hooks**: agents MUST document **parameters** (inputs), **return value** (output shape), and **side effects** (subscriptions, fetches, global store updates)—all three for public hooks.
- Agents MUST call out **stale closure** risks or **dependency array** rationale only when non-idiomatic.

### Styles and layout

- Agents MUST comment **non-obvious** layout decisions, theme tokens, or responsive breakpoints that encode product requirements—not every CSS property.

## Anti-patterns (MUST NOT)

- Agents MUST NOT leave **commented-out code** in commits (use version control instead).
- Agents MUST NOT add **TODO** without owner or ticket reference when the work is non-trivial (prefer a tracked issue).
- Agents MUST NOT add **redundant** comments that duplicate symbol names or types (`// increment i`).

## Task completion gate (REQUIRED)

Agents MUST verify before finishing the task:

- New or changed public surfaces have appropriate module, component, or API documentation.
- **Inputs and outputs** are documented for public functions, handlers, components, and hooks per the **Inputs and outputs (REQUIRED)** section above.
- Comments match current behavior; no misleading or copy-pasted text.
- Backend docstrings or equivalent align with one consistent style per repository (see overlay for project defaults if any).
- Frontend components and hooks used across features document props, rendered or exposed behavior, and hook return values—not only high-level intent.
- **AI-friendly patterns (REQUIRED)** are satisfied for every changed module and public surface, including **`Role:`** / **`Not in this file:`** / **`Key dependencies:`** / **`See also:`** on module files (or the **Trivial files** one-paragraph exception).
